<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>com.barezen</groupId>
    <artifactId>barezen-parent</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath>../../pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>barezen-web</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>BareZen Web Contract</name>
  <description>
    The HTTP contract: request, response, headers, handler, and the route table that maps a method and a path to
    a handler. Defines what a web component receives and returns without naming a server. Contains no middleware,
    no servlet and no transport code.
  </description>

  <!--
    Deliberately no <maven.compiler.release> override here. JDK 17 is the compatibility baseline of
    the whole framework and is owned by the parent. See ADR-0002.
  -->

  <!--
    No compile-scope dependency at all, including none on barezen-core. This is the phase's point: the HTTP
    contract is a set of value types and one signature, and a contract that needed the lifecycle engine, the
    assembler or a logging facade could not be implemented by a server that knows nothing about them
    (ADR-0018). barezen-core remains the allowed direction of dependency - core <- web - but nothing in the
    contract has a use for core yet, so the edge is not declared. When an HTTP server adapter is written as a
    LifecycleParticipant it will need core, and that module, not this one, will declare it.

    Phase 2.8 qualifies that paragraph rather than replacing it: the compile scope is still empty, and the test
    classpath now carries barezen-core, to prove that a Plugin can register routes through constructor injection and
    be driven by the real LifecycleOrchestrator (ADR-0019 §15). The distinction is load-bearing rather than cosmetic:

      - barezen-web.jar still ships with zero dependencies, which is what an adapter and an application
        consume, so nothing about the contract's portability changed;
      - the two architecture rules that read the package (the_contract_depends_only_on_java_lang_and_java_util
        and the_contract_does_not_reach_into_core) import with DoNotIncludeTests, so they measure the shipped
        surface and stay green - which is also exactly why a violation of them would be invisible in a test;
      - a main source file that imports com.barezen.core fails to compile, because test scope does not reach
        the compile classpath. The boundary is enforced by javac, not by a rule.

    There is still no logback dependency, and adding one was the other option here. barezen-core logs on startup,
    so the plugin test's JVM prints SLF4J's "no providers were found" notice to stderr and defaults to a no-op
    logger. That is the honest outcome rather than a problem to paper over: nothing in barezen-web logs, so a
    binding on its test classpath would only make the contract's test run quieter by giving it an implementation to
    write to, and a three-line notice is a better price for that than a dependency a reviewer has to ask about.

    Test dependencies otherwise mirror barezen-core: the same JUnit, AssertJ and ArchUnit versions come from
    barezen-parent.
  -->
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.barezen</groupId>
      <artifactId>barezen-core</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
      <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.assertj</groupId>
      <artifactId>assertj-core</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
      <artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
        <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>prepare-agent</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>prepare-agent</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
          <execution>
            <id>report</id>
            <phase>verify</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>report</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>

  <profiles>
    <profile>
      <!--
        Same gate and same JDK 21 binding as barezen-core (ADR-0002). The contract is four public types with
        no unreachable platform path in them, so there is no reason here - unlike the virtual thread provider -
        for a lower bar on JDK 17.
      -->
      <id>coverage-gate</id>
      <activation>
        <jdk>[21,)</jdk>
      </activation>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
            <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            <executions>
              <execution>
                <id>check-coverage</id>
                <phase>verify</phase>
                <goals>
                  <goal>check</goal>
                </goals>
                <configuration>
                  <rules>
                    <rule>
                      <element>BUNDLE</element>
                      <limits>
                        <limit>
                          <counter>LINE</counter>
                          <value>COVEREDRATIO</value>
                          <minimum>0.85</minimum>
                        </limit>
                      </limits>
                    </rule>
                  </rules>
                </configuration>
              </execution>
            </executions>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>
  </profiles>
</project>
